      "dom_id": "question-54451",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-4)}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡(x+4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250624/assets/57f95a683f7f9c06287bfed1bc459df33dbcdfcdf253d6878e0c77de60d906e0.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-4.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -4}{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
