      "dom_id": "question-67654",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250624/assets/52817b9c0ede04e8e920de8a52ebe025916f314cc937097c7fb91ea612e18f1b.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-1.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
